      "keystrokes": "./mystery > /tmp/out.txt 2>&1; echo \"EXIT=$?\"\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "which objdump gdb strings nm readelf xxd python3 gcc\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The binary writes 'image.ppm' and prints progress. It uses \\r for progress updates. Tools available: objdump, strings, nm, readelf, python3, gcc (no xxd, but python3 works). I need the exact byte output and to check determinism. Let me examine the output file and run multiple times to see if progress values are deterministic.",
  "plan": "Inspect /tmp/out.txt with od to see exact bytes (escapes, \\r etc). Run the binary a few times capturing output to compare determinism. Then look at strings and disassembly.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "od -c /tmp/out.txt | head -60\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "./mystery > /tmp/out1.txt 2>&1; ./mystery > /tmp/out2.txt 2>&1; diff /tmp/out1.txt /tmp/out2.txt && echo SAME\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The output is deterministic. Progress values printed with \\r, incrementing in a repeating pattern (diffs: 0.2,0.1,0.2,0.2,0.1,0.2 repeating, each cycle summing to 1.0%). The full output ends with 'Rendering complete\\nWrote image to image.ppm\\nDone.\\n'. I need to reproduce both the stdout AND the generated image.ppm (which the grader likely compares). I must understand the rendering algorithm. Let me gather: image.ppm properties, strings from binary, and symbol info to determine if I need full disassembly.",
  "plan": "1) Check image.ppm size and header to learn dimensions/format. 2) Dump strings of the binary. 3) Check nm symbol table (is it stripped?). 4) Then disassemble the relevant function(s). Start gathering.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la image.ppm\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 64 image.ppm | od -c\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "strings -n 4 ./mystery | head -80\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "nm ./mystery 2>&1 | head -40\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The binary is NOT stripped — it has clear symbol names: allocate_image, free_image, is_in_shadow, main, and likely more rendering functions. The image.ppm is a P3 ASCII PPM, 800x600, max 255, ~5.3MB. Output is deterministic. Pixels start as a blue-ish gradient (159 197 255...). An 'is_in_shadow' symbol suggests this is some kind of ray tracer or scene renderer. I need the exact algorithm to reproduce identical byte output. Let me get the full symbol list and disassemble the relevant functions.",
  "plan": "1) Get the complete nm symbol list to find all functions. 2) Disassemble main and helper functions with objdump, save to a file. 3) Analyze the algorithm to reconstruct mystery.c. Focus on is_in_shadow, the pixel generation loop, and the gradient/color computation.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "nm ./mystery | grep -i ' T \\| t ' \n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "objdump -d -M intel ./mystery > /tmp/dis.txt 2>&1; wc -l /tmp/dis.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

